================================================================================
C2-dba-any: why it has no MAYBE population, and what it is actually matching
2026-11-03
================================================================================

The observation (dev/ADJ-ROUND-STAGE1-20261103.log point 1): between the Oct-25
and Nov-3 builds of the same grant population, C2-dba-any went from 1,457
firings with 1,384 MAYBE to 458 firings with 0 MAYBE, while still firing.

This file is written after three wrong turns, two of them mine and recorded
here because each looked like an answer: see WHAT I GOT WRONG at the end.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. FIRST, A CORRECTION TO HOW THE REBUILD WAS FRAMED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
dev/ADJ-ROUND-STAGE1-20261103.log says the rebuild ran "under npnormalize 0.1.9
... so only the match differs". That is wrong, and so was this section's first
attempt at correcting it, which said the reference layout differed because
"the Oct-25 reference carried 41 columns against the current 38". It did not:
41 is the Nov-3 stamp's own count (38 on disk plus the 3 recovery keys added in
flight), so that was one reference counted at two moments of the SAME run. See
dev/COMPARABILITY-PLAN.md section 2.

What is true, read today:

  * The Oct-25 artifact carries NO run stamp at all -- no normalizer, no
    reference, no config. Nothing about that run can be compared to anything.
  * It matched against the bundle built 2026-08-21 under v1-6dcb298522cc5118,
    pre-split npmatch. dev/REF-REBUILD.log said so at the time: "the
    triangulation run (population cb3c78ff0f1a9546) [was] matched against the
    old bundle ... a rerun will not reproduce [it]".
  * np_signature_history() puts EIGHT signatures between the two runs, ending
    at v1-5e901add4a04a5d6 (npnormalize 0.1.8).
  * Direct evidence that the keys moved: identical input both runs (596,539
    grant rows, population cb3c78ff0f1a9546), yet 91,690 identities in October
    against 91,008 in November. The dedup key is name_key plus street_key, so
    only the normalizer can do that.

So the normalizer DID move, and this log's earlier claim that nothing was
attributable to it is withdrawn. Sections 2 and 3 are measured on the current
frame alone and do not depend on it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. THE CURRENT STATE, MEASURED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Frame-wide, over all 458 identities whose best candidate comes from C2:

  score 0.600  x457
  score 0.980  x1
  review floor 0.650   auto-accept 0.780   (npmatch::np_config()$thresholds)

So C2-dba-any currently yields ONE usable match in 458. Everything else sits
0.05 below the review floor, at a single constant value.

300 of those identities were rematched with the pairs retained, 1,272 C2 pairs:

  score      min 0.600, max 0.600 -- one distinct value
  zip5       zero on 100.0% of pairs
  geo_state  zero on 100.0%
  geo_zip5   zero on 100.0%
  city       zero on  99.8%

The geo columns are zero because the pass crosses state lines on purpose. That
is what it is for, not a defect. The consequence is that it earns no geographic
credit, so its score is name credit alone.

The one that clears: CANOE CREEK K8 PTA, ST CLOUD -> "Canoe Creek K8 Pta",
EIN 86-3264768, score 0.980, margin 0.900, n_close 1. The grant's state is
recorded MO and Canoe Creek K-8 is in St. Cloud, Florida -- a distinctive name
with unusable geography, which is precisely the case an unblocked pass exists
for, and it resolved. The pass is not worthless. It is badly targeted.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. WHAT IT IS ACTUALLY MATCHING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
npmatch's name_key over the 458 identities -- the key C2 joins to the
reference's dba_key:

  YMCA                        54      JCC                          4
  SALVATION ARMY              29      NCJW                         4
  YOUNG LIFE                  13      SUSAN G KOMEN FOR THE CURE   4
  SPCA                         9      COUNCIL ON AGING             3

  top 10 keys account for 126 of 458 (27.5%)
  name_key is 1-4 characters on 212 of 458 (46.3%); median 5
  digits-only on 4 (0.87%); any digit at all on 10 (2.18%)

That is the finding. C2-dba-any is a pass whose population is short generic
names and national brands -- acronyms (YMCA, SPCA, JCC, MDA, NCJW) and
federated names (SALVATION ARMY, YOUNG LIFE) -- matched to some organisation's
trade name somewhere in the country with no geography to choose between the
hundreds of local entities carrying it. Nearly half its queries are four
characters or fewer. Those cases are not nearly-resolvable; they are
unresolvable, and 0.600 is the right answer for them.

npmatch's own comment at R/score.R:106 says this about the DBA side, having
found it independently:

  "A DBA is frequently a short generic trade name whose rarity says nothing"

with the worked example of a recipient "literally named 1" whose legal name is
SLOVAK GYMNASTIC UNION SOKOL -- the same shape as THE 1421 FOUNDATION, which
appeared in the sampled pairs matching six unrelated EINs that each carry
dba "1421".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. HOW THE OLD 1,384 REACHED REVIEW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every one of them scored exactly 0.720. That is not a coincidence and not a
name similarity:

  npmatch::np_config()$distinct_name_floor_xstate == 0.72

They were not clearing the review floor on their merits. They were being
FLOORED UP to it by the distinctive-name cross-state promotion
(npmatch R/score.R: `score[promote] <- pmax(score[promote], floor_val[promote])`,
where floor_val is distinct_name_floor_xstate when geo_state != 1).

The promotion's premise is "this exact legal name is rare in the reference, so
an exact match on it is trustworthy". For a C2 match that premise is read off
the wrong string: name_freq describes the reference row's PRIMARY name, which
is rare (YOUNG MENS CHRISTIAN ASSOCIATION OF GREATER <CITY> appears once),
while the match was made on its DBA, which is "YMCA" and says nothing. So
1,384 nationwide acronym matches were promoted into review on the rarity of
names that had no part in making them.

The promotion is now withheld and the raw 0.600 stands, which is correct.

WHICH CONDITION STOPPED HOLDING IS NOT ESTABLISHED. `promote` requires
name_score >= 0.95, name_freq <= distinct_name_maxfreq (3), `informative`
(disabled by default, min_idf 0), and `on_primary`. `on_primary` is the one
that should block a DBA match, and it is the likeliest answer -- but
distinct_name_main_only and the name_ver columns it reads both date from
5e23269, 2026-08-20, so both were already present for the Oct-25 run, and that
alone does not explain a change in late October. Settling it needs the pairs
rescored with distinct_name_main_only = FALSE, which was not done.

Not knowing the trigger does not weaken sections 2 and 3: what the pass finds,
and that it cannot clear the floor on 457 of 458, are measured directly.

ADDED 2026-11-03, after section 1 was corrected: there is now a mechanism worth
testing, which there was not when this was written. `promote` requires
name_freq <= distinct_name_maxfreq (3) -- the normalized PRIMARY name must be
rare in the reference. Folding spellings together makes names LESS rare, and
the distinct-name count has fallen at every rebuild:

  2,797,423   Aug-21 bundle, the one the Oct-25 run matched against
  2,797,111   Oct-26 rebuild   (dev/REF-REBUILD.log)
  2,796,717   Nov-2 rebuild, current (dev/REF-REBUILD-20261103.log)

npnormalize 0.1.7 folding eleven spellings of FOUNDATION is exactly the shape
of change that moves a name from name_freq 3 to 4 and withdraws the promotion.
This is a better candidate than on_primary, because unlike
distinct_name_main_only it dates from the window in question. Still untested:
it needs the retained pairs rescored, or name_freq read for the 1,384 names
against both bundles.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. WHERE THE AFFECTED ORGANISATIONS WENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1,354 of the 1,384 traced into the new run by name + city + state (NOT by id --
see pfmatch-identity-ids; an id join across two runs returns base rates):

  new pass            MAYBE    NO
  A2-name-state         383     0
  A3-name-dba            17     0
  C1-name-any             1   388
  C2-dba-any              0   367
  B1-token-state          1   133
  B2-token-concat         2    49
  D1-acronym              4     7
  (none)                  0     2

400 moved onto STATE-BLOCKED EXACT passes and stayed in review -- strictly
better evidence than the unblocked join they had. The remaining ~950 have
nothing above the floor from any pass, so C2's 0.600 is the best on offer and
nothing better was displaced.

Same EIN picked before and after: 332 of 1,352 (24.6%).
Median best-candidate score 0.720 -> 0.600.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. THE ONLY DIRECT EVIDENCE ON WHETHER THESE WERE WORTH REVIEWING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
data-dev/AUDIT-MAYBE-100.csv against dev/maybe-verdicts.csv, older pass naming,
top picks from the dba pass:

  exact-name-dba  n=6  CORRECT_TOP 0  CORRECT_DEEPER 1  AMBIGUOUS 4  NO_CORRECT 1

Zero of six had the right answer as the top pick. n=6 carries no precision
claim, and that naming does not separate the blocked dba pass from the
unblocked one. Consistent with the above; not proof of it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. CONCLUSION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Not a regression. 1,384 nationwide acronym matches were being floored into
review by a rarity argument computed on a string that played no part in the
match. They stopped being, and 400 of the underlying organisations were picked
up by stronger state-blocked passes in the process.

What remains is a pass that pays for itself once in 458 and, the other 457
times, supplies the near-miss a reviewer sees on a NO row -- a nationwide
acronym match to an unrelated organisation.

Dropping it would cost the one real catch (CANOE CREEK K8 PTA: distinctive
name, unusable geography). Keeping it as-is costs 457 misleading near-misses.
The lever that separates those two is name distinctiveness, which the one catch
has and all 457 lack -- 46.3% of them are four characters or fewer. npmatch
already carries the parameter for it, distinct_name_min_idf, currently 0 and
disabled; its own note says it was measured at "only 2 matches, net zero" on
990-PF grants, but that was measured as a gate on the PROMOTION, not as a gate
on admitting a query to an unblocked pass at all. Those are different tests and
only the first has been run. See pfmatch-truncation-negative, which already
names the distinctive-name floor as the real unexamined lever.

OPEN, in order of value:
  1. Gate C2-dba-any's QUERY side on name distinctiveness (token IDF or
     character length) rather than dropping the pass. Expected: keeps the
     CANOE CREEK class, drops the YMCA class. Measure the tier mix either side.
  2. Establish which `promote` condition stopped holding (section 4), by
     rescoring retained pairs with distinct_name_main_only = FALSE. Until then
     the same promotion may still be firing on other DBA passes.
  3. A3-name-dba / A4-dba-name / A5-dba-dba join on the same dba_key but are
     state-blocked, so they DO earn geographic credit and are not stuck at
     0.600. A spurious promotion there is worse, not better. Unexamined.
  4. C1-name-any is also unblocked and carries 388 NO near-misses on the traced
     set. Its scores were not examined.

Reference-side note, measured but not load-bearing: dba_key is digits-only on
39,746 of 948,422 non-empty rows (4.2%, 12,848 distinct values, one reaching
126 reference rows) and digit-leading on 32.6% ("2200804 FRUITLAND LITTLE
LEAGUE", "64 HURON COUNTY POMONA" -- BMF group/subordinate codes). Real
pollution, but it is NOT what C2 is matching on: only 4 of its 458 queries have
a digits-only name_key.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT I GOT WRONG, since each wrong turn looked like the answer
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
a) "The reference's dba_key collapsed." Measured: 948,422 non-empty in both
   bundles, 782,996 -> 782,986 distinct. The reference side did not move.

b) "The 0.720 came from npmatch a5726e7, where empty fields scored 1.0 against
   each other." That commit is real and its own message says exactly that, and
   the arithmetic looked right. But 0.720 is distinct_name_floor_xstate to the
   digit, which is a floor and not a weighted sum, so the empty-field story is
   not what put those pairs at 0.720. Reported before it was checked.

c) "C2 is matching BMF group-exemption numbers." Drawn from THE 1421 FOUNDATION
   matching six EINs with dba "1421" -- a real pair, and 1 of only 4 such
   queries in 458 (0.87%). The population is acronyms, not numbers. A vivid
   single case was generalised without counting first.

d) A first attempt at section 5 joined the two runs on grant_id and produced
   "6 of 400 cases in common" and "182 of the old 400 are now YES" -- both base
   rates, because the ids are positions in two different runs. See
   pfmatch-identity-ids.

e) "The two runs differ in the reference layout: 41 columns against 38." Both
   numbers are the Nov-3 run's -- 38 as saved, 41 as matched after
   pf_run_batches() adds the three recovery keys. A within-run difference was
   reported as a between-run one, in the very section written to correct
   somebody else's framing. The real between-run difference is that the Oct-25
   run recorded nothing at all; see section 1 as it now stands.

